```

---

## 6️⃣ Running the app

1. **Create a new React project** (if you don’t already have one):

   ```bash
   npx create-react-app my-react-table-app
   cd my-react-table-app
   ```

2. **Replace** the auto‑generated files with the ones above:

   * `public/index.html`
   * `src/index.js`
   * `src/App.js`
   * `src/styles.css`

3. **Install dependencies** (React + React‑DOM are already installed by CRA).

4. **Start the dev server**:

   ```bash
   npm start
   ```

   The app will open in `http://localhost:3000` and you should see a nicely formatted table showing the numbers from the 2‑D array.

---

## 7️⃣ Extending the demo

* **Dynamic editing** – add a form or inline editing to change cell values.
* **Resize the matrix** – expose controls to add/remove rows or columns.
* **Persist data** – wire in `localStorage` or a backend API.
* **Styling** – use CSS modules, Tailwind, or a UI framework (MUI, Ant Design).

Feel free to tweak the array or the rendering logic – this scaffold gives you a clean starting point for any project that needs 2‑D array manipulation in React. Happy coding!

Below is a **complete, copy‑and‑paste ready** example of a sign‑up form that asks for:

| Field | HTML input type | JavaScript / HTML5 validation |
|-------|-----------------|-------------------------------|
| First name | `text` | `required` |
| Last name | `text` | `required` |
| Email address | `email` | `required` + native email pattern |
| Phone number | `tel` | `required` + pattern |
| Password | `password` | `required` + pattern |
| Confirm password | `password` | `required`, custom *match* checker |

The examples use **HTML5 validation** for the obvious checks, and a small **JavaScript snippet** for the *“password === confirm”* test plus a nice user‑friendly error message. CSS styles are intentionally simple but can be adapted to your design.

---

## 1. Full Code (single file)

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
<meta charset="UTF-8">
<title>Sign‑Up Form</title>
<style>
  /* Basic form styling */
  body {font-family: Arial, sans-serif; background:#f5f5f5; margin:0; padding:2rem;}
  .signup-form{max-width:400px; margin:auto; background:#fff; padding:2rem;
                border-radius:8px; box-shadow:0 0 10px rgba(0,0,0,.1);}
  label{display:block; margin-top:1rem;}
  input{width:100%; padding:0.5rem; margin-top:0.3rem;
        border:1px solid #ccc; border-radius:4px;}
  .error{color:#D8000C; font-size:0.9rem; margin-top:0.2rem;}
  button{margin-top:1.5rem; padding:0.8rem;
         background:#28a745; color:#fff; border:none; border-radius:4px;
         width:100%; font-size:1rem; cursor:pointer;}
  button:hover{background:#218838;}
</style>
</head>
<body>

<form id="signupForm" novalidate class="signup-form">
  <h2>Sign Up</h2>

  <label for="firstName">First Name <span aria-hidden="true">*</span>
    <input type="text" id="firstName" name="firstName" required>
    <small class="error" id="firstNameError"></small>
  </label>

  <label for="lastName">Last Name <span aria-hidden="true">*</span>
    <input type="text" id="lastName" name="lastName" required>
    <small class="error" id="lastNameError"></small>
  </label>

  <label for="email">Email <span aria-hidden="true">*</span>
    <input type="email" id="email" name="email" required>
    <small class="error" id="emailError"></small>
  </label>

  <label for="phone">Phone Number <span aria-hidden="true">*</span>
    <input type="tel" id="phone" name="phone"
           pattern="^\+?[0-9]{10,15}$" required>
    <small class="error" id="phoneError"></small>
  </label>

  <label for="password">Password <span aria-hidden="true">*</span>
    <input type="password" id="password" name="password"
           pattern="(?=.*\d)(?=.*[a-z])(?=.*[A-Z]).{8,}"
           title="At least 8 characters, one uppercase, one lowercase, one number" required>
    <small class="error" id="passwordError"></small>
  </label>

  <label for="confirmPassword">Confirm Password <span aria-hidden="true">*</span>
    <input type="password" id="confirmPassword" name="confirmPassword" required>
    <small class="error" id="confirmPasswordError"></small>
  </label>

  <button type="submit">Register</button>
</form>

<script>
document.addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded', function() {
  const form = document.getElementById('signupForm');

  // Helper to show / hide error messages
  const showError = (input, message) => {
    const errorElm = document.getElementById(input.id + 'Error');
    errorElm.textContent = message;
  };
  const clearError = (input) => {
    const errorElm = document.getElementById(input.id + 'Error');
    errorElm.textContent = '';
  };

  // Basic live input validation
  form.querySelectorAll('input').forEach(input => {
    input.addEventListener('input', () => {
      if (input.checkValidity()) clearError(input);
    });
  });

  form.addEventListener('submit', function(e) {
    e.preventDefault(); // Stop real submission for demo

    let valid = true;

    // 1️⃣  Native HTML5 checks
    if (!form.checkValidity()) {
      // Find first invalid field and show its message
      const firstInvalid = form.querySelector(':invalid');
      if (firstInvalid) {
        firstInvalid.focus();
        firstInvalid.reportValidity(); // triggers native UI
      }
      valid = false;
    }

    // 2️⃣  Custom checks – password match
    const pw = form.password.value;
    const cpw = form.confirmPassword.value;
    if (pw !== cpw) {
      showError(form.confirmPassword, 'Passwords do not match');
      valid = false;
    } else {
      clearError(form.confirmPassword);
    }

    if (!valid) return;

    // 3️⃣  Successful form → send data somewhere (demo alert)
    const formData = new FormData(form);
    const userData = Object.fromEntries(formData.entries());
    console.log('Submitted data', userData);
    alert('✅ Registration successful!'); // Replace with real submit logic
  });
});
</script>

</body>
</html>
```

### What just happened?

| Section | How it helps |
|---------|--------------|
| **HTML** | Uses semantic tags (`<label>`, `<small>`), `required`, and patterns to enforce rules even before JS runs. |
| **CSS** | Provides a clean layout and instant visual cue for errors. |
| **JavaScript** | Performs cross‑field checks (password confirmation) and displays custom error messages without a page reload. |

---

## 2. Customization Tips

| Want to change? | Where to edit |
|-----------------|---------------|
| **Phone pattern** – `pattern="^\+?[0-9]{10,15}$"` | Adjust the RegEx to fit your local spec. |
| **Password rules** (at least 8 chars, 1 upper, 1 lower, 1 digit) | Edit the `pattern` attribute or the JS logic. |
| **Error messages** | Functions `showError / clearError` currently target a `<small>` tag next to the input. |
| **Submit action** | Replace the `alert()` with an AJAX `fetch()` to your backend. |
| **Style** | Modify the CSS block – add brand colors, fonts, or responsive media queries. |

### Quick “real‑world” example

```js
// Replace the alert when the form is valid
if (valid) {
  fetch('/api/signup', {
    method: 'POST',
    headers: {'Content-Type': 'application/json'},
    body: JSON.stringify(userData)
  })
  .then(r => r.ok ? alert('Welcome!') : Promise.reject(r))
  .catch(err => alert('Oops! Something went wrong.'));
}
```

---

## 3. Testing Your Form

1. **Open the file** in a modern browser (Chrome, Edge, Firefox, Safari).
2. Try leaving a field blank – you’ll see the browser’s built‑in tooltip.
3. Input an invalid email – “Please include an '@' …”.
4. Enter a short password – the pattern will block it.
5. Type different strings into password / confirm password – the custom JS error will appear.
6. Once everything passes, the success alert will appear (you can open the console to see the JSON data).

---

## 4. Accessibility & Best Practices

- **`<label for="id">`** – pairs the text with the input, enabling screen‑reader navigation.
- **`aria-hidden="true"`** on the asterisks to avoid duplication for screen readers.
- **`novalidate`** on the form disables *native* constraint validation only if you want full custom handling, but here we've left it to complement our JS.
- **Password confirmation** uses an explicit comparison instead of a pattern to keep logic readable.

---

## Wrap‑up

You now have a fully functional **HTML/CSS/JS** sign‑up form with:

- **Client‑side validation** (HTML5 + JS)
- **Responsive layout** (pure CSS)
- **Clear error messaging**
- A solid basis to hook into any backend of your choice.

